<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danube</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Danube is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s great rivers, flowing from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> it served as a major frontier and line of defence, while in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> it functioned as an important route for trade and communication. Its lower reaches also marked a zone of contact with steppe peoples, a theme noted in the profile of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danube.jpg" alt="Danube" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Visions of Domino · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Gell%C3%A9rt_Hill_to_the_Danube,_Hungary_-_Budapest_(28493220635).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
